emerhub.bali|https://www.instagram.com/p/DV-uXn5Encw/|2026-03-17|carousel|6|Nyepi is the Balinese Hindu New Year, and it's unlike any other new year celebration in the world.\\n\\nThe night before, on Pengerupukan, giant demon effigies called Ogoh-Ogoh are paraded through the streets with loud music, drums, and torches. These figures represent evil spirits. At the end of the night, they're burned.\\nThe purpose is to draw out and purify negative energy from the community.\\n\\nThen comes Nyepi. The entire island goes completely silent for 24 hours. No lights, no travel, no work, no noise.\\nThe idea is that any remaining evil spirits will think Bali is empty and leave.\\nFor everyone on the island, it's a day of stillness and reflection.\\n\\nThis year, Nyepi falls on Thursday, March 19. Whether you're visiting, living here, or running a business, you'll need to prepare in advance.\\nSwipe through for everything you need to know.||Nyepi is the Balinese Hindu New Year, and it's unlike any other new year celebration in the world.|hidden|0||17:12|fonte=dom ; like_count hidden (ikon tanpa angka) ; bahasa=en\
